Digitally reconfigured dependencies are resources and capabilities associated principally with technology-enabled practices or constraints that technology can partly mitigate. Persistently established dependencies condition both traditional and digital practices without technological mitigation. Culture-related nodes are excluded.
Digitally reconfigured dependencies
1. Secure, site-adaptive and interoperable construction-data architecture (primary count=32; supporting counts=16 and 8). Heritage modelling faced fragmented formats, safety analytics required machine-processable accident reports, and bid automation depended on centralized records (c001M01N46;c008M01N47;c009M01N39). The architecture must combine standardized formats and semantic interfaces with privacy controls, because external APIs created confidentiality risks and engineering narratives required restricted access (c009M01N49;c012M01N44). Rural transmission failures, inaccurate sensors and congested sites further require infrastructure adaptable to field conditions (c001M01N44;c017M01N25;c005M02N46).
2. Task-specific and learner-responsive XR training design and implementation capability (count=25). Effective implementation requires realistic hazard scenarios, suitable interfaces, consequence feedback and assessment of transfer to site behaviour. Immersion alone was insufficient: one excavation system conveyed only explicit knowledge, while mixed-reality instruction could overload working memory (c004M01N33;c010M01N35). Bridge and confined-space cases required practical hazard assessment and visible consequences (c005M02N56;c011M02N40). Controller unfamiliarity and participant withdrawal also demonstrate the need for differentiated ergonomic and instructional support (c005M01N43;c005M01N44).
3. Digitally reconfigured expert roles in content creation, contextual interpretation, knowledge curation and output validation (count=20). Digital tools supplemented rather than replaced construction judgement: bid-generation systems could not interpret nuanced risks without experts, and generated proposals still required iterative review (c009M01N44;c009M01N22). Narrative repositories required specialists to elicit and structure experience, while XR systems required time-consuming scenario development (c012M02N65;c005M02N53). Deployment therefore depends on protected expert time, digital competence and formal validation responsibilities.
Persistently established dependencies
1. Jurisdiction-specific construction-safety regulatory compliance architecture (count=14). Australian fall-prevention rules, PRC confined-space regulations and Peruvian OHS legislation structured training content, equipment selection and risk assessment (c002M02N35;c011M01N32;c016M01N33). Digital delivery does not remove these obligations.
2. Sustained financial, computing-platform and usable project-data resource base (count=12). Immersive training required a CAVE facility, accident classification required a substantial historical corpus, and bid automation required integrated information infrastructure (c003M01N23;c008M01N40;c009M01N38). Deployment remains conditional on stable funding, maintained platforms and sufficiently complete data.
3. Formal standards for model quality, safety induction, ethics and information governance (count=9). Heritage digitization applied graded modelling standards, while excavation VR required regulatory objectives, ethics approval and participant induction (c001M02N25;c004M02N28). These controls support legitimate and consistent knowledge retention.
4. Continuous participation capacity of construction experts, facilitators and trained employees (count=9). Safety onboarding, trained narrative facilitators and staff-retention arrangements supplied the expertise needed to elicit and validate experiential knowledge (c002M02N34;c012M01N33;c009M02N54). Technology cannot retain knowledge without competent and continuous human participation.
Together, persistent dependencies provide regulation, resources, governance and participation; digitally reconfigured dependencies convert these foundations into interoperable data, situated learning and expert-validated knowledge assets.